{
  "$about": "A curated catalog of models worth running on ONE machine: an RTX 3090 (24 GB) with 32-64 GB of RAM, 2026. This file is DATA. src/catalog.mjs loads it, does the arithmetic and decides fit; nothing here is executable.",
  "$units": "`file` is the size of that quant's download: one file as Hugging Face reports it, or the sum of its parts when the quant is split across files (Llama-3.3-70B Q8_0 is two). Decimal gigabytes (1e9 bytes), which is how Hugging Face prints a file size; `params` is billions of parameters; `context` and `serveContext` are tokens. Every `file` figure was read from the Hugging Face API's own blob size for that exact file, not estimated.",
  "$fit": "`context` is what the model was trained for. `serveContext` is the context worth configuring on a 24 GB card, and it is the one the KV arithmetic uses. They differ on purpose: a model trained at 262144 tokens would need hundreds of gigabytes of KV cache at that length, so quoting the trained length as the requirement would make every entry look impossible. `layers` and `kvDim` (key/value heads x head dimension) come from each model's config.json and are what turn a context length into bytes.",
  "$honesty": "Sizes are shown as they are, not as they would flatter the card. An entry whose Q8_0 does not fit in 24 GB keeps its Q8_0 quant and says so in `notes`; `url` is null where no resolve URL was verified rather than a guessed one. Every resolve URL in this file was checked against huggingface.co (HTTP 302 to the CDN) when the catalog was written.",
